WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:04.683
An X R P Ledger account belongs to whoever can sign for it.

2
00:00:04.683 --> 00:00:09.072
Nobody at a help desk can reset it, and no payment can be undone.

3
00:00:09.072 --> 00:00:12.177
So security comes down to protecting the keys.

4
00:00:13.877 --> 00:00:17.930
Most accounts are emptied the same way: the secret is exposed.

5
00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:23.683
It is typed into a fake site, saved in a cloud note or a screenshot, or read by malware.

6
00:00:23.683 --> 00:00:26.101
The ledger itself is not broken into.

7
00:00:27.801 --> 00:00:31.460
One: keep the master seed offline, on paper or steel.

8
00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:35.119
Nobody legitimate, including NOSHASHI, ever needs it.

9
00:00:35.569 --> 00:00:39.302
Two: sign day to day with a regular key held in a hardware wallet.

10
00:00:39.752 --> 00:00:46.195
Three: require several signers for a treasury, and then disable the master key, or the list can be bypassed.

11
00:00:46.645 --> 00:00:53.279
Four: close the doors strangers use, such as unsolicited N F T offers, checks and payment channels.

12
00:00:54.979 --> 00:00:56.500
Here is a real account.

13
00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:01.593
It sent payments of one drop, with a memo pointing to a fake free X R P site.

14
00:01:02.043 --> 00:01:05.935
Then it deleted itself into the next account, which did the same.

15
00:01:05.935 --> 00:01:13.840
Every account in the series was made to end in xaman, the name of a real wallet, so a hurried copy from history picks the wrong one.

16
00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:20.306
The habit that beats it: take addresses from your own saved records, never from your transaction history.
